To show that \( U(r, \theta) \) is harmonic inside the circle \( |z| = 1 \), we use the method of contour mapping. The function \( U(r, \theta) \) is defined in polar coordinates as \( u(x, y) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \right) \). The function \( U(r, \theta) \) is analytic inside the unit circle \( |z| = 1 \), so it is harmonic inside the circle.

Thus, the function \( U(r, \theta) \) is harmonic inside the circle \( |z| = 1 \). 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned*}
\frac{\partial U}{\partial r} &= \frac{2}{\pi} \left( \frac{2 \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} + 2r \sin \theta \cdot \frac{2r}{(1 - r^2)^2} \right) \\
&= \frac{4 \sin \theta}{2\pi (1 - r^2)} + \frac{4r^2 \sin \theta}{2\pi (1 - r^2)^2} \\
= \frac{2 \sin \theta}{2\pi (1 - r^2)} + \frac{2r^2 \sin \theta}{2\pi (1 - r^2)^2},
\quad \text{and} \\
\frac{\partial U}{\partial \theta} &= \frac{2}{\pi} \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \cdot \frac{1}{\cos^2 \theta} = \frac{4r \sin \theta}{(1 - r^2) \pi \cos^2 \theta}.
\end{align*}
}
The function \( U(r, \theta) \) is harmonic if \( \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial r^2} + \frac{\1}{4} \frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial \theta^2} = 0 \). 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned*}
\frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial r^2} &= \frac{2}{\pi} \left( \frac{4r}{(1 - r^2)^2} + \frac{4 \sin \theta (1 - r^2) + 2r \sin \theta (2r)}{(1 - r)^2)^2} \right) \\
&= \frac{2}{\pi} \left( \frac{4r}{(1 - r^2)^2} + \frac{4 \sin \theta - 4 r^2 \sin \theta + 4r^2 \sin \theta}{(1 - r^2)^2} \right) \\
&= \frac{2}{\pi} \left( \frac{4r + 4 \sin \theta}{(1 - r^2)^2} \right) = \frac{8r + 8 \sin \theta}{\pi (1 - r^2)^2},
\quad \text{and} \\
\frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial \theta^2} &= \frac{4r}{(1 - r^2)} \cdot \frac{1}{\pi \cos^2 \theta} - \frac{4 \sin \theta}{2\pi \cos^2 \theta} \cdot \frac{2

